\subsection*{Before We Begin}
An episodic task is a learning problem in which the internal state of the model is reset at the beginning of each input sequence, so that each sequence is processed independently.  During training the model sees sequences up to a certain maximum length, while during testing it may be asked to handle longer sequences that were never seen before.  To “generalise” means that the model can correctly handle these longer test sequences even though it was only trained on shorter examples.  Understanding these ideas lets us see why the results reported for the NTM are significant.

\section*{Motivation}
Imagine sorting a pile of mail by urgency: you first drop each letter into a slot labelled with its priority (low, medium, high). Later you walk along the slots in order and collect the letters, producing a sorted batch.  Researchers created a series of small algorithmic problems—copying a sequence, repeating a copy, associative recall, dynamic N‑grams, and priority sort—to ask whether a neural model can learn the underlying procedures rather than merely memorising examples.  The goal is to reveal whether the Neural Turing Machine can acquire loop‑like and table‑like behaviours that let it work on inputs far larger than those used for training.

\section*{Intuition}
The NTM augments a neural controller with an external memory that can be written to and read from at each time step.  In the copy task, the controller stores each incoming symbol in a fresh memory location, then later steps through those locations in order to reproduce the sequence.  Because the same write‑once‑read‑once pattern can be repeated arbitrarily many times, the NTM behaves like a simple loop that does not depend on the length it was trained on.  Similar ideas underlie the other tasks: a nested loop can repeat a copy, a content‑based lookup plus a fixed offset can retrieve the next item, a rewritable table can count N‑gram frequencies, and writing according to a numeric priority yields a sorted read‑out.

\section*{Formal Definition}
Consider a set of five benchmark tasks:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Copy}:  For an input sequence $[x_{1},x_{2},\dots ,x_{T}]$ the model must output the identical sequence.  The NTM writes each $x_{t}$ to a distinct memory slot $M_{t}$ and later reads from $M_{1},M_{2},\dots ,M_{T}$ in that order.
\item \textbf{Repeat Copy}:  The model receives an input sequence together with an integer $R$ indicating how many times the sequence should be reproduced.  The NTM executes an outer loop $R$ times, each time iterating through the previously stored memory slots.
\item \textbf{Associative Recall}:  A list of items $[a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{N}]$ is presented, followed by a query item $q$.  The required output is the item that immediately follows $q$ in the list.  The NTM stores a compressed representation of each $a_{i}$, performs a content‑based lookup to locate the memory slot containing $q$, then reads the slot that is one position forward.
\item \textbf{Dynamic N‑Grams}:  At each time step the model observes a symbol from a stream generated by an unknown $6$‑gram distribution and must predict the next symbol.  The NTM treats its memory as a table that records counts of observed $6$‑grams; this table can be overwritten as new data arrive, thereby implementing a Bayesian‑like estimator.
\item \textbf{Priority Sort}:  Each input vector arrives with an associated scalar priority $p\in[0,1]$.  The NTM writes the vector into a memory address proportional to $p$ (e.g., low $p$ near the beginning, high $p$ near the end).  After all inputs are stored, the read head scans the memory from low to high addresses, producing the vectors sorted by priority.
\end{enumerate}

In the copy and repeat‑copy tasks the NTM’s behavior reproduces an \emph{array‑iteration loop} and a \emph{nested loop}, respectively.  In associative recall, the combination of \emph{content‑based addressing} (finding the slot that matches the query) and a fixed \emph{location offset} (moving one slot forward) yields the correct subsequent item.  For dynamic N‑grams, the external memory functions as a \emph{re‑writable frequency table}, allowing the model to adapt instantly to a new distribution.  In priority sort, the strategy of writing according to the scalar priority and then reading sequentially implements a genuine sorting procedure rather than a fixed mapping.

\section*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Problem statement:}  Perform the copy task on the short input sequence $[a,b,c]$ and show that the same steps work for the longer, unseen sequence $[d,e,f,g,h]$.

\textbf{Step 1 – Initialization.}  
At time $t=0$ the memory is empty and the read and write heads are ready to address the first location.

\textbf{Step 2 – Write phase for $[a,b,c]$.}  
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=1$: the controller receives symbol $a$, writes $a$ to memory slot $M_{1}$, and moves the write head to the next slot.
\item $t=2$: the controller receives symbol $b$, writes $b$ to $M_{2}$, and moves the write head forward.
\item $t=3$: the controller receives symbol $c$, writes $c$ to $M_{3}$, and moves the write head forward.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3 – Read phase for $[a,b,c]$.}  
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=4$: the read head points to $M_{1}$, reads $a$, and outputs $a$.
\item $t=5$: the read head moves to $M_{2}$, reads $b$, and outputs $b$.
\item $t=6$: the read head moves to $M_{3}$, reads $c$, and outputs $c$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 4 – Write phase for the longer sequence $[d,e,f,g,h]$.}  
The same rule (“write the current input symbol to the next free memory slot”) is applied:
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=1$: write $d$ to $M_{1}$.
\item $t=2$: write $e$ to $M_{2}$.
\item $t=3$: write $f$ to $M_{3}$.
\item $t=4$: write $g$ to $M_{4}$.
\item $t=5$: write $h$ to $M_{5}$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 5 – Read phase for $[d,e,f,g,h]$.}  
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=6$: read $M_{1}$ → output $d$.
\item $t=7$: read $M_{2}$ → output $e$.
\item $t=8$: read $M_{3}$ → output $f$.
\item $t=9$: read $M_{4}$ → output $g$.
\item $t=10$: read $M_{5}$ → output $h$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Final answer:}  The NTM reproduces $[a,b,c]$ correctly and, without any change to its internal algorithm, also reproduces the unseen longer sequence $[d,e,f,g,h]$.

\textbf{What this shows us:}  The copy task does not rely on memorising a fixed‑length pattern; instead the NTM implements a loop that writes each input to a fresh memory cell and later reads the cells sequentially.  Because the loop rule is independent of the sequence length, the model generalises to arbitrarily long inputs.

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
The NTM learns algorithmic looping and memory‑manipulation strategies that let it handle sequences far longer than those seen during training, whereas a standard LSTM degrades because it lacks such explicit external memory operations.

